#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda} .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N.$ This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda \\neq 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m = 9\\lambda.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda x}+B{e}^{9\\lambda x}+9B\\lambda x{e}^{9\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda }+B{e}^{9\\lambda }+9B\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 9A\\lambda +B+9B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{18\\lambda }+2B{e}^{18\\lambda }+2(9A\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }+B{e}^{18\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(9A\\lambda +B+18B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+18A\\lambda +2B+36B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+(4+36\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/assets/29df2dce71db62482d1664cc2f4033dce94574fb1903d80828f4f1d367f8c9a9.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+9\\lambda )-(1+9\\lambda )(1+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -(1+18\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -1-18\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19  \\textrm{ and } \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A  + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A = 0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C = B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{ and } Ce^{x/2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b910453-fc2a-5ed9-ad33-a358bef8bd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos (2λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164743} {:answer/id #uuid "f42dfafd-3188-5cca-b8f2-632471a08c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos ((λ_{n}x)/(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164744} {:answer/id #uuid "00f8352c-2e93-5bb2-8d65-ef085d92c19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin (λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164745} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4521af-5bc2-508b-9cb6-dbefc6f5d27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin ((λ_{n}x)/(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164746} {:answer/id #uuid "1330d445-d1e2-58b3-b58d-05aa89e35b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin (2λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164747} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b217d-1601-5f20-a990-f727907f7858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos (λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1330d445-d1e2-58b3-b58d-05aa89e35b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin (2λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164747} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5b6d7-8028-531d-bed3-9255d161fbf8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4607304-a98c-5e83-b6d1-eb02787d636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164750} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5329c8-d05b-52a0-aafe-ac5a75fb4260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164751} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a0b08-b29c-5002-8683-df45359791f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164752} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b0d9b-555a-50a5-916a-92f9072a48b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(nπ)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164753} {:answer/id #uuid "7408ac64-c03d-588e-b58d-1b73fc52e6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b0d9b-555a-50a5-916a-92f9072a48b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(nπ)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164753} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bd56b0-f643-5799-951e-5039e368bdc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fd0718d-5c1d-56bf-aa5a-76fcf93883d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338667" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ef94ca-e262-53ba-8102-52c69558cab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374589} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb47315-9240-58e5-b725-aafd8a30e359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374590} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeb00fe-e627-5cb6-8a92-8b3ef46b80b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374591} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8f73e-a6f3-54a4-8775-5081daa8f11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bb47315-9240-58e5-b725-aafd8a30e359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374590} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ba240b-a4e6-5ceb-a8cb-1d7e6d116ecd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab19195e-dd4d-5e7b-b0ad-c0bba2ada72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374594} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbed0c-5e08-54e4-8329-0629aa9978d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{2x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374595} {:answer/id #uuid "7c03a967-7c2a-5429-9869-2b13f1c7b1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374596} {:answer/id #uuid "643941f4-5bcd-5b85-b25d-89b45682c75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c03a967-7c2a-5429-9869-2b13f1c7b1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374596} :answer-field/id #uuid "579fd51d-9d23-5a14-baa7-eeb55f3e1121" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338729" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ba17bf-c8a7-50d8-addb-789f7e63e8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374600} {:answer/id #uuid "edbe8ed7-d3d8-57e9-9324-55582f348a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374601} {:answer/id #uuid "46914119-61c0-55e3-b0b1-9ba8f00a5b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374602} {:answer/id #uuid "5d30216a-c2b4-5a27-aa70-4e3cc4f526ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374603} {:answer/id #uuid "8f544337-0e9f-5861-a470-ac7d7749ebd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d30216a-c2b4-5a27-aa70-4e3cc4f526ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374603} :answer-field/id #uuid "468ce62f-1113-5665-85a3-5c884c570372" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374599} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2777bc12-e5ee-5651-97a1-9f500bcf66d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{7x/6}$ and $Cx{e}^{-7x/6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374606} {:answer/id #uuid "9caccf85-2557-57cc-9fef-93abdcc03be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374607} {:answer/id #uuid "402b4be1-fdcb-5321-95f9-d09764532176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374608} {:answer/id #uuid "c0670a1b-b4c8-534a-9097-3801f5691eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374609} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8e4b95-91b7-541d-b14a-1f2fb08145f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0670a1b-b4c8-534a-9097-3801f5691eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374609} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ffc179-bde8-5331-9899-6419a9455487" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fbda52c-e557-5901-ad9a-c542b15bd0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338853" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6\\lambda {y}^{′}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-6\\lambda m+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-3\\lambda )(m-3\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=3\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{3\\lambda x}+3B\\lambda x{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2B{e}^{6\\lambda })+3(B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 4B{e}^{6\\lambda }+3B{e}^{6\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{6\\lambda }(7+18\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{6\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-7x/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7b67d9-d212-5aed-8b91-5cc27baefc0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{16}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374613} {:answer/id #uuid "d6560db5-5ee8-59cd-a56e-ae19633ffb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374614} {:answer/id #uuid "f03a4ae5-96aa-55cc-9ed5-b8e246db8bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374615} {:answer/id #uuid "25634c73-2313-5b4d-a2ea-762153017936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f03a4ae5-96aa-55cc-9ed5-b8e246db8bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374615} :answer-field/id #uuid "98aa0f9d-989c-5234-b1e3-1d00886a9525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8314215f-d6f4-57f8-bc53-ad2b4b13d3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374618} {:answer/id #uuid "30f03516-51d0-5e46-aac3-94bf6b5bdbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374619} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b59f7f-0b67-575b-84c9-c639e18b88c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374620} {:answer/id #uuid "7268cbe5-27b3-5b41-989e-517375f9fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7268cbe5-27b3-5b41-989e-517375f9fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374621} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef48e50-bb94-517c-8741-e6b3a64b9b36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46ccc891-cde1-5fd1-bac2-5a6521022f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-338900" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e408e244-93a5-5153-ae8f-5a34db29684a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374624} {:answer/id #uuid "0710e9b5-45f4-555d-9c51-8526e3539b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374625} {:answer/id #uuid "02ebf14a-62f8-59ba-9dae-75782a9881c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374626} {:answer/id #uuid "53048c9f-4a12-5e1d-8602-d7946b83a6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ebf14a-62f8-59ba-9dae-75782a9881c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374626} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8bfa53-bacb-592a-9570-c0b42e4de6d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1809112-54a8-5c1e-8d90-53afba6039ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374629} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e25f0b-ebad-5ddd-8d45-e914cde44dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374630} {:answer/id #uuid "943988ef-b9fc-5281-9019-734b52ce21e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374631} {:answer/id #uuid "9887355e-f22b-5da2-aef0-23f7686a150b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9887355e-f22b-5da2-aef0-23f7686a150b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374632} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9dc26a-3f27-5dc0-92f2-273dd7b13cf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75e4f01-4a1b-5592-b071-be5563b560e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-338904" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9839477-e38c-5cb5-bb25-3e65dcddf912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374635} {:answer/id #uuid "0b544697-4fab-5f86-95c0-bc2309db050d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374636} {:answer/id #uuid "d0318f22-a11a-591e-a3da-d958a22da882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{20}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374637} {:answer/id #uuid "894f549a-38bd-50f1-8f76-2ade6089c22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b544697-4fab-5f86-95c0-bc2309db050d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374636} :answer-field/id #uuid "edebe1cb-a803-503b-80a9-256983cc55ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf3e695-aff2-55cb-950c-697c0f43e6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374640} {:answer/id #uuid "82f414f1-5879-572f-839e-fb0e4582d0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374641} {:answer/id #uuid "544224e4-62b0-5a57-8f74-dcc83c20fbd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374642} {:answer/id #uuid "28672692-f324-5ee4-8765-0bac338463d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf3e695-aff2-55cb-950c-697c0f43e6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374640} :answer-field/id #uuid "deedf306-a48d-5fd0-a7ea-825b8d69d73c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" :question/math-academy-id "q-338905" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fd2cbd-6d53-5354-8027-16362316797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374646} {:answer/id #uuid "3182a8cd-b165-5e56-82af-af6dadc64769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374647} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a12f4f-0483-5a03-8fe9-f0752ce2fc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374648} {:answer/id #uuid "612980ed-947b-5df8-8b15-be575a3ec2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23fd2cbd-6d53-5354-8027-16362316797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374646} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0509504-292b-5654-bc80-25b98a9e65d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d8c5d06-5e17-5a98-8449-203de18bb4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374651} {:answer/id #uuid "cb08637a-3507-502f-bb35-3a143a0a8c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374652} {:answer/id #uuid "374fa734-7446-5bd1-bf71-64c583bcb57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374653} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ed5428-3d25-525f-9f95-304fc2f04acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb08637a-3507-502f-bb35-3a143a0a8c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374652} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace2a4c4-d72b-5b81-a42a-7c78257a08fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea62eee-bb57-5794-b427-82156c628984" :question/math-academy-id "q-338915" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n6\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e4f3869-feae-5b56-9d43-69fd273dc9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374657} {:answer/id #uuid "0f250e22-0d82-5f1f-aa6b-b21875b8b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374658} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a923df-d100-5ecf-b6d4-8df33f5082db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374659} {:answer/id #uuid "7c99f66a-fea1-5385-8ca8-bfcff6747677" :answer/type {:db/id